PROMPT 10 — HUSH 1.0 (release)

Goal. Ship HUSH 1.0: feature-complete on Linux + Windows, macOS at parity, installers, docs, and a stable schema.

Contracts to lock.

1. Schema v2 is frozen at 1.0. Any post-1.0 schema change requires a formal migration path and version bump.
2. No feature is claimed that isn't in the limitations list as implemented. Release notes must be exhaustive.
3. macOS backend choice (CoreAudio; AU plugin format optional in 1.1, not 1.0) recorded.
4. Documentation is part of the release gate, not an afterthought: user manual, quick-start, voice-command reference, plugin-hosting guide, interchange fidelity guide.
5. Signed installers for Linux (AppImage or flatpak — record choice), Windows, macOS.

Implementation.

1. macOS port (CoreAudio backend, IPC, threading, file I/O) at full parity.
2. Documentation set.
3. Installers for three platforms.
4. Final performance profiling pass; published baseline specs.
5. Final validation report across all gates since 0.1.0.

Gates.

· Every gate from 0.1.0 through 0.9 passes on Linux, Windows, and macOS in CI.
· Cross-platform render comparison: same project → bit-identical output on all three platforms.
· Installers produce working installs on all three platforms.
· Full documentation set complete and reviewed.
· Limitations list is exhaustive and truthful.
· A new engineer can follow quick-start to a working first session in ≤15 minutes on each platform.

Deliverables. Source ZIP, three signed installers, documentation set (manual, quick-start, voice reference, plugin guide, interchange guide), 1.0 architecture spec (frozen), full validation report, release notes.

Post-1.0 (do not start). AU plugins, CLAP, Pro Tools .ptx import, Ableton .als import, cloud collaboration, remote control apps. These are 1.1+ and require their own prompt sequence.

---

How to run this sequence

1. One prompt at a time. Do not start Prompt N+1 until every gate in Prompt N has passed and the decision log is updated.
2. Gates are binding. If a gate fails, the milestone does not ship. Extend it. Do not weaken the gate.
3. Decision log is cumulative. Every milestone appends. It is the institutional memory of why choices were made.
4. Limitations list is cumulative and honest. Never remove an item unless it's actually implemented.
5. Contracts in §2 of each prompt are binding. Implementation may refine; it may not contradict.
6. When in doubt, stop and ask. Record the open question in the decision log with options considered.

Definition of done for the whole sequence: HUSH 1.0 installed and running on Linux, Windows, and macOS; every gate since 0.1.0 passing on all three; exhaustive and truthful limitations list; frozen schema v2; and a documentation set a new user can complete a first session from in 15 minutes.

